---
/**
 * Track 11 (Neural Network Intuition), Lesson 1, illustration 2 of 3.
 *
 * Why a hand-written rule for "3" breaks. One reasonable rule (two rounded
 * bumps, stacked on the right, open left) is stated at the top. Below it,
 * three real-world threes each violate it in a different way: a steeply
 * slanted 3, a flat-topped 3, and a fast 3 whose lower bump is a straight
 * flick. An accent ring marks the offending feature on each. The annotation
 * carries the teaching; the glyphs are deliberately stylized.
 */

const panels = [
	{
		cx: 160,
		// upright base 3, then slant the whole group
		d: 'M 30 40 C 30 16 94 16 94 50 C 94 78 60 80 52 82 C 60 84 94 86 94 116 C 94 150 30 150 30 124',
		transform: 'translate(28,0) skewX(-20)',
		ring: { x: 132, y: 92, rx: 34, ry: 30 },
		lines: ['Slanted: the two bumps sit', 'off to the side, not stacked.'],
	},
	{
		cx: 400,
		// flat top instead of a rounded upper bump
		d: 'M 30 24 L 92 24 L 92 52 C 92 80 60 80 52 82 C 60 84 94 86 94 116 C 94 150 30 150 30 124',
		transform: '',
		ring: { x: 61, y: 24, rx: 40, ry: 18 },
		lines: ['Flat top: the upper section', 'is more flat than round.'],
	},
	{
		cx: 640,
		// fast flick: lower "bump" is a near-straight diagonal
		d: 'M 30 40 C 30 16 94 16 94 50 C 94 78 60 80 52 82 L 92 152',
		transform: '',
		ring: { x: 74, y: 120, rx: 30, ry: 38 },
		lines: ['Fast: the lower bump is', 'really just a straight flick.'],
	},
];
const glyphY = 18;
---

<figure class="cw-illus">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 800 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="nn-rules-title nn-rules-desc">
		<title id="nn-rules-title">One rule for the digit 3 fails on three real handwritten threes</title>
		<desc id="nn-rules-desc">At the top, a stated rule: a 3 has two rounded bumps, stacked on the right, open to the left. Below are three handwritten threes that each break it. The first is steeply slanted, so its bumps sit off to the side rather than cleanly stacked. The second has a flat top instead of a rounded upper bump. The third was written fast, so its lower bump is a straight flick rather than a curve. A violet ring highlights the offending feature on each. The rule that seemed reasonable misses all three.</desc>

		<text x="400" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="currentColor"><tspan font-weight="600">The rule:</tspan> two rounded bumps, stacked on the right, open left.</text>

		{panels.map((p) => (
			<g>
				<g transform={`translate(${p.cx - 60}, ${glyphY}) ${p.transform}`}>
					<path d={p.d} fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
				</g>
				<ellipse cx={p.cx - 60 + p.ring.x} cy={glyphY + p.ring.y} rx={p.ring.rx} ry={p.ring.ry} fill="none" stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
				<text x={p.cx} y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12.5" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.85">{p.lines[0]}</text>
				<text x={p.cx} y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12.5" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.85">{p.lines[1]}</text>
			</g>
		))}
	</svg>
	<figcaption>One honest rule, three real threes, three misses. You could patch the rule for slant, then for flat tops, then for flicks, but every patch invites a new 3 you did not plan for. The rules pile up and never quite close the gap. That is the signal you are reaching for the wrong tool.</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
	.cw-illus { margin: 1.75rem auto; max-width: 100%; }
	.cw-illus svg { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 720px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto; }
	.cw-illus figcaption { text-align: center; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic; }
</style>
